  ('1336', 'Dialysis Technology', 3, $ahs${"academic_year": 3, "subjects": [{"subject_no": "Paper I", "title": "Dialysis Technology", "mode": "flat", "topics": ["DIALYSIS TECHNOLOGY - In the teaching of dialysis technology, stress shall be laid on basic principles of the Subject with more emphasize on its applied aspects.", "Goal: The broad goal of the teaching of undergraduate students in dialysis technology aims at Providing an in-depth knowledge of hemodialysis and peritoneal and peritoneal dialysis Therapy.", "Objectives (Knowledge): (a) Comprehend the various modalities of renal replacement therapy with knowledge of Merits and demerits of each; (b) Comprehend the principles of hemodialysis and peritoneal dialysis; (c) Learn how to offer dialytic therapy for renal failure patients; (d) Learn the various forms of hemodialysis and when each is to be applied; (e) Learn to manage complications of dialysis therapy; (f) Learn dialysis therapy in various special groups of patients e.g., unstable patients in The intensive care unit, children, cardiac patients etc; (g) Learn plasmapheresis, Continuous therapies; (h) Manage anticoagulation on patients on dialysis; (i) Measure the adequacy of dialysis; (j) Adminster various drugs in emergencies and as a routine; (k) Use blood transfusion", "Objectives (Skills): (a) Start and close hemodialysis sessions independently; (b) Successfully cannulate arterio-venous fistulae for hemodialysis; (c) Train patients and their caregivers in performing peritoneal dialysis; (d) Do water maintenance for the hemodialysis room; (e) Maintain hemodialysis machines with respect to regular disinfection; (f) Operate hemodialysis machines, CAPD cylers reuse machines independently; (g) Participate in Conduct of renal transplant programme; (h) Use pulse oximeters, pressure monitors defibrillators correctly", "SYLLABUS - The course in dialysis technology is to provide an understanding of the various forms of renal replacement therapy and successful performance of the same in patients with renal Failure.", "TEACHING SCHEDULE - PART I: 1. History, types of Dialysis,", "PART I: 2. Principles of Dialysis, quantification of adequacy", "PART I: 3. Dialysis Team-rights-responsibilities-patient doctor relationship", "PART I: 4. Dialysis reuse", "PART I: 5. Dialyser Membranes", "PART I: 6. Vascular Access - Temporary & Permanent", "PART I: 7. Equipment - Accessories - Function", "PART I: 8. Computer applications in Dialysis", "PART I: 9. Dialysate delivery system", "PART I: 10. Composition of dialysate", "PART I: 11. High flux / high efficiency dialysis", "PART I: 12. Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy / Slow Low Efficiency Dialysis", "PART I: 13. Complications in dialysis patients", "PART I: 14. Water treatment-pre treatment, deionizer, Reverse Osmosis", "PART I: 15. Dialysis in Neonates, infants & children", "PART I: 16. Renal data maintenance (Teaching Hours - 20 Hrs.)", "PART II: 1. Machine and patient monitoring during hemodialysis", "PART II: 2. Patient Assessment - Pre, intra & post dialysis", "PART II: 3. Lab data analysis", "PART II: 4. Acute and chronic dialysis prescription", "PART II: 5. Medications in dialysis patients", "PART II: 6. Nutrition management in dialysis patients", "PART II: 7. Anticoagulation", "PART II: 8. Infection control and universal precautions", "PART II: 9. Phychosocial aspects & patient education", "PART II: 10. Quality assurance in dialysis", "PART II: 11. Complications of hemodialysis - Acute & chronic", "PART II: 12. Acute and Chronic Peritoneal Dialysis", "PART II: 13. History, access, physiology of Peritoneal Dialysis", "PART II: 14. PD - Transport kinetics, ultrafiltration, UF, Intermittent PD, Continuouos Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis, Automated Peritoneal Dialysis, Dialysis Solutions, Novel uses of PD", "PART II: 15. Infectious and non infectious complications of PD", "PART II: 16. Renal transplant co-ordination (Recipient and donor workup, psychosocial and legal aspects, cadaver donor Maintenance, principles of post operative management and follow-up)", "1 Preparation of dialysis patients for various surgical procedure and post operative Dialysis support", "2 Basic and advanced cardiac life support"], "units": [], "reference_books": ["Clinical text of Nephrology By John Fegally", "Text book of Nehrology - Oxford and Brenner Rector (Reference only)", "Textbook of Dialysis therapy - Nissenson (Reference only)", "Textbook of Peritoneal Dialysis - Ram Gokal (Reference only", "Handbook of dialysis - John T. Daugirdas", "NANT and Oxford - Text books of Dialysis for technologist", "List of Journals for dialysis therapy: Journal of Indian society of Nephrology", "List of Journals for dialysis therapy: Journal / News Letter for Dialysis Technologist and Nurses", "List of Journals for dialysis therapy: Nephrology, Dialysis, Transplantation", "List of Journals for dialysis therapy: Seminars in Dialysis", "List of Journals for dialysis therapy: Seminars in Nephrology"], "notes": "III Year Paper I - Dialysis Technology (Teaching Schedule Part I and Part II). The 'List of books for dialysis therapy' and 'List of Journals for dialysis therapy' appear at the end of the III Year syllabus and are attached here as reference material for the dialysis papers."}], "intro": "III Year Paper I - Dialysis Technology (Teaching Schedule Part I and Part II). The 'List of books for dialysis therapy' and 'List of Journals for dialysis therapy' appear at the end of the III Year syllabus and are attached here as reference material for the dialysis papers."}$ahs$::jsonb, $ahs${"components": [{"name": "Internal Assessment (IA)", "max": 50, "min": 25}, {"name": "Theory", "max": 100, "min": 50}, {"name": "Practical", "max": 50, "min": 25}, {"name": "Viva", "max": 50, "min": 25}], "question_pattern": {"duration_hours": null, "sections": [], "total_marks": 100}, "notes": "Scheme of Examination (B.Sc. Dialysis Technology). I Year: each paper has IA (Max 50 / Min 25), Theory (Max 100 / Min 50), Practical (Max 50 / Min 25); Viva not applicable in I Year. II Year: IA (Max 50 / Min 25), Theory (Max 100 / Min 50); Practical and Viva not applicable. III Year: IA (Max 50 / Min 25), Theory (Max 100 / Min 50), Practical (Max 50 / Min 25), Viva (Max 50 / Min 25). English and Computer are internal papers (I Year) with IA Max 50/Min 25 and Theory Max 100/Min 50 - there is no university examination for English and Computer; marks are sent to the university (these are not listed in the DT paper list). Internal Assessment: 'Wherever there is no Log Book/Project/Record work the 10 mark be added to the Practical of the respective subject.' Question pattern duration and section breakup not specified in source."}$ahs$::jsonb, $ahs${"total_duration": "ONE YEAR COMPULSORY ROTATORY INTERNSHIP", "postings": [{"area": "MAINTENANCE HEMODIALYSIS", "duration": "8 MONTHS"}, {"area": "ICU DIALYSIS", "duration": "2 MONTHS"}, {"area": "CONTINUOUS AMBULATORY PERITONEAL DIALYSIS", "duration": "2 MONTHS"}], "notes": "One year compulsory rotatory internship as stated after the I Year scheme of examination."}$ahs$::jsonb),
  ('1337', 'Renal Disease Therapeutics', 3, $ahs${"academic_year": 3, "subjects": [{"subject_no": "Paper II", "title": "Renal Disease Therapeutics", "mode": "flat", "topics": ["RENAL DISEASE THERAPEUTICS - In the teaching of concepts of disease and clinical evaluation of patients, stress shall be Laid on basic principles of the subject with more emphasize on its applied aspects.", "Goal: The broad goal of the teaching of undergraduate students in concepts of disease and Clinical evaluation aims at providing an introduction to various kidney disease and their Evaluation.", "Knowledge: At the end of the course the student shall be able to: (a) Comprehend the various presentations of kidney diseases; (b) Learn how to diagnose and evaluate patients with various disease conditions like Acute renal failure, nephrotic / nephritic syndrome, urinary tract infection, Asymptomatic urinary abnormalities, Chronic Kidney Disease (especially stage v) renal stone diseases, obstructive nephropathies, congenital & inherited renal diseases, pregnancy associated renal diseases, renal vascular disorders and hypertension associated renal diseases; (c) Learn to order appropriate test towards confirmation of diagnosis; (d) Learn to initiate therapy in each of these conditions; (e) Learn the appropriate time of referral to nephrology services in each of the conditions; (f) Screening for renal diseases in the community and hospital patients.", "Skills: At the end of the course the student shall be able to; (a) Collect medical history from patients with various kidney diseases; (b) Clinically examine patients with kidney diseases and order appropriate Investigations; (c) Write rational prescriptions for patients with kidney diseases.", "SYLLABUS - The course in concepts of disease and clinical evaluation is to provide an understanding Of the nature of various diseases and evaluation of the same.", "1 Acute renal failure", "2 Nephrotic syndrome - primary & secondary", "3 Nephritic syndrome", "4 Urinary Track Infection - urinary track infections", "5 Asymptomatic urinary abnormalities", "6 Chronic Kidney Disease", "7 Renal stone diseases", "8 Obstructive nephropathies", "9 Congenital & inherited renal diseases", "10 Pregnancy associated renal diseases", "11 Renal vascular disorders & hypertension associated renal diseases"], "units": [], "reference_books": [], "notes": "III Year Paper II - Renal Disease Therapeutics (concepts of disease and clinical evaluation). Reference books/journals for dialysis therapy are listed once at the end of the III Year syllabus and are recorded under Paper I (code 1336)."}], "intro": "III Year Paper II - Renal Disease Therapeutics (concepts of disease and clinical evaluation). Reference books/journals for dialysis therapy are listed once at the end of the III Year syllabus and are recorded under Paper I (code 1336)."}$ahs$::jsonb, $ahs${"components": [{"name": "Internal Assessment (IA)", "max": 50, "min": 25}, {"name": "Theory", "max": 100, "min": 50}, {"name": "Practical", "max": 50, "min": 25}, {"name": "Viva", "max": 50, "min": 25}], "question_pattern": {"duration_hours": null, "sections": [], "total_marks": 100}, "notes": "Scheme of Examination (B.Sc. Dialysis Technology). I Year: each paper has IA (Max 50 / Min 25), Theory (Max 100 / Min 50), Practical (Max 50 / Min 25); Viva not applicable in I Year. II Year: IA (Max 50 / Min 25), Theory (Max 100 / Min 50); Practical and Viva not applicable. III Year: IA (Max 50 / Min 25), Theory (Max 100 / Min 50), Practical (Max 50 / Min 25), Viva (Max 50 / Min 25). English and Computer are internal papers (I Year) with IA Max 50/Min 25 and Theory Max 100/Min 50 - there is no university examination for English and Computer; marks are sent to the university (these are not listed in the DT paper list). Internal Assessment: 'Wherever there is no Log Book/Project/Record work the 10 mark be added to the Practical of the respective subject.' Question pattern duration and section breakup not specified in source."}$ahs$::jsonb, $ahs${"total_duration": "ONE YEAR COMPULSORY ROTATORY INTERNSHIP", "postings": [{"area": "MAINTENANCE HEMODIALYSIS", "duration": "8 MONTHS"}, {"area": "ICU DIALYSIS", "duration": "2 MONTHS"}, {"area": "CONTINUOUS AMBULATORY PERITONEAL DIALYSIS", "duration": "2 MONTHS"}], "notes": "One year compulsory rotatory internship as stated after the I Year scheme of examination."}$ahs$::jsonb),
  ('1601', 'Anotomy, physiology, Biochemistry', 1, $ahs${"academic_year": 1, "subjects": [{"subject_no": "Paper I", "title": "Anotomy, physiology, Biochemistry", "mode": "units", "topics": [], "units": [{"unit_no": "Anatomy - Unit 1", "topics": ["Introduction to Human Anatomy - Definitions, planes of the body, organ system, optometry", "Tissues of the body - epithelium, connective tissue, bone and cartilage.", "Types of cells and their arrangements"]}, {"unit_no": "Anatomy - Unit 2", "topics": ["Skeleton system - skull bones, other bones and body joints", "Muscles - Different types - origin, insertion, functions and nerve supply", "Cardiovascular system - heart & blood vessels"]}, {"unit_no": "Anatomy - Unit 3", "topics": ["Digestive system - mouth, esophagus, stomach, small and large intestine, rectum, anus, liver, spleen, pancreas", "Respiratory system - nose, bronchi, lungs, diaphragm", "Endocrine system: - Major endocrine glands", "Urinary system - kidneys, urinary bladder, urethra (male and female)", "Reproductive system - Male and female"]}, {"unit_no": "Anatomy - Unit 4", "topics": ["Nervous system - path, cell types, blood-brain barrier, reflex arc, cranial, spinal and peripheral nerves, autonomic nervous system", "Brain: - Parts, protective coverings, cerebrospinal fluid, brain stem, diencephalons, Cerebellum.", "Skin and appendages / sense organs - anatomy"]}, {"unit_no": "Physiology - Unit 1", "topics": ["Introduction to physiology - Cell structure, body fluid compartments, Homeostasis, transport across cell membrane, neuromuscular junction and muscle contraction", "Blood - composition and function of blood, erythropoiesis, anaemia, polycythemia, blood coagulation, plasma proteins, blood groups including Rh", "Cardiovascular system - Conduction of heart beat, cardiac cycle, ECG, cardiac output, arterial blood pressure measurement, heart rate."]}, {"unit_no": "Physiology - Unit 2", "topics": ["Respiration - Mechanism, lung volume and capacities, transport 